    "markdown": "---\r\ntitle: Probability\r\naudience: CBSE Class 10\r\nsubject: Mathematics\r\nchapter_number: 14\r\nchapter_slug: probability\r\nstatus: draft\r\ncontent_type: chapter-pack\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Probability\r\n\r\nThis is an original teaching draft for Class 10 Mathematics, Chapter 14. The chapter is small, but students need clean thinking about outcomes and favourable cases.\r\n\r\n## chapter goal\r\n\r\nBy the end of this chapter, a student should be able to:\r\n- understand what probability means\r\n- identify outcomes and favourable outcomes\r\n- find theoretical probability in simple experiments\r\n- use the range `0` to `1` correctly\r\n- solve basic card, coin, and dice problems\r\n\r\n## teacher note on textbook alignment\r\n\r\nThe current NCERT Class 10 chapter strongly focuses on:\r\n- experimental idea of chance\r\n- theoretical probability for equally likely outcomes\r\n- simple events from coins, dice, cards, and everyday situations\r\n\r\n## chapter flow for the platform\r\n\r\n1. Meaning of probability\r\n2. Outcomes and events\r\n3. Theoretical probability formula\r\n4. Range and interpretation\r\n5. Simple applications\r\n6. Quick revision and practice\r\n\r\n## 1) meaning of probability\r\n\r\nProbability measures how likely an event is to happen.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n- getting head on one coin toss\r\n- getting a `6` on a die\r\n- drawing a red card from a deck\r\n\r\n## 2) outcomes and events\r\n\r\nAn outcome is one possible result.\r\n\r\nFor one coin toss:\r\n- `H`\r\n- `T`\r\n\r\nAn event is what we want to happen.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nEvent = getting head\r\n\r\nFavourable outcomes are the outcomes that satisfy the event.\r\n\r\n## 3) theoretical probability\r\n\r\nIf all outcomes are equally likely, then:\r\n\r\n`Probability of an event = Number of favourable outcomes / Total number of outcomes`\r\n\r\n### example 1: die\r\nProbability of getting `4` on a die:\r\n\r\nFavourable outcomes = `1`\r\nTotal outcomes = `6`\r\n\r\nSo probability = `1/6`\r\n\r\n### example 2: coin\r\nProbability of getting tail on one coin toss:\r\n\r\n`1/2`\r\n\r\n### example 3: cards\r\nProbability of drawing a king from a standard deck of `52` cards:\r\n\r\nFavourable outcomes = `4`\r\n\r\nProbability = `4/52 = 1/13`\r\n\r\n## 4) range of probability\r\n\r\nProbability always lies between `0` and `1`.\r\n\r\n- `0` means impossible event\r\n- `1` means certain event\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n- probability of getting `8` on a normal die = `0`\r\n- probability of getting a number less than `7` on a die = `1`\r\n\r\n## 5) complement idea\r\n\r\nIf probability of an event is `P(E)`, then probability of not happening is:\r\n\r\n`1 - P(E)`\r\n\r\nThis is useful when \"not\" type questions are easier than direct counting.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\nProbability of not getting a head on one coin toss:\r\n\r\n`1 - 1/2 = 1/2`\r\n\r\n## 6) exam-friendly summary notes\r\n\r\n### must-know facts\r\n- Probability is favourable outcomes divided by total outcomes.\r\n- The formula works for equally likely outcomes.\r\n- Probability is always between `0` and `1`.\r\n- Impossible event has probability `0`.\r\n- Certain event has probability `1`.\r\n\r\n### one-line revision points\r\n- count carefully\r\n- favourable over total\r\n- answer cannot be negative or above `1`\r\n\r\n## 7) common mistakes students make\r\n\r\n1. Forgetting that the sample space must contain all possible outcomes.\r\n2. Counting favourable outcomes wrongly.\r\n3. Giving a probability greater than `1`.\r\n4. Using the formula when outcomes are not equally likely without checking.\r\n5. Mixing up event and outcome.\r\n\r\n## 8) board tips\r\n\r\n- Write the sample space if the question is small.\r\n- State favourable outcomes and total outcomes separately.\r\n- Simplify the fraction at the end.\r\n- Check whether the answer is between `0` and `1`.\r\n\r\n## 9) how to remember the chapter\r\n\r\n### memory box 1\r\nProbability is a fraction of success cases out of all cases.\r\n\r\n### memory box 2\r\nNo chance means `0`.\r\n\r\n### memory box 3\r\nSure chance means `1`.\r\n\r\n### memory box 4\r\nIf answer is bigger than `1`, something is wrong.\r\n\r\n## 10) solved practice set\r\n\r\n### question 1\r\nFind the probability of getting an even number on one die.\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\nEven numbers are `2, 4, 6`\r\n\r\nSo probability = `3/6 = 1/2`\r\n\r\n### question 2\r\nFind the probability of getting a red card from a deck.\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\nThere are `26` red cards in `52` cards.\r\n\r\nProbability = `26/52 = 1/2`\r\n\r\n### question 3\r\nWhat is the probability of getting a number greater than `6` on a normal die?\r\n\r\nAnswer:\r\nImpossible event, so probability = `0`\r\n\r\n## 11) short answer practice\r\n\r\n1. What is an outcome?\r\n2. What is an event?\r\n3. When does the basic probability formula apply?\r\n4. Why must probability lie between `0` and `1`?\r\n5. What does probability `1` mean?\r\n\r\n## 12) mini quiz with answers\r\n\r\n### quiz\r\n1. Probability of getting head on one fair coin toss is:\r\n   - a) `0`\r\n   - b) `1/2`\r\n   - c) `1`\r\n   - d) `2`\r\n\r\n2. Total outcomes on one die are:\r\n   - a) `2`\r\n   - b) `4`\r\n   - c) `6`\r\n   - d) `8`\r\n\r\n3. Probability of an impossible event is:\r\n   - a) `-1`\r\n   - b) `0`\r\n   - c) `1/2`\r\n   - d) `1`\r\n\r\n4. Probability of drawing a king from `52` cards is:\r\n   - a) `1/52`\r\n   - b) `4/52`\r\n   - c) `13/52`\r\n   - d) `26/52`\r\n\r\n5. A valid probability can be:\r\n   - a) `3/2`\r\n   - b) `-1/4`\r\n   - c) `2`\r\n   - d) `3/5`\r\n\r\n### answer key\r\n1. b\r\n2. c\r\n3. b\r\n4. b\r\n5. d\r\n\r\n## 13) content style note for future lessons\r\n\r\nThis chapter should become short example-led lessons:\r\n- sample space basics\r\n- die and coin problems\r\n- card problems\r\n- complement idea\r\n\r\nStudents benefit most from repeated counting examples here.\r\n\r\n## comic style recap\r\n\r\nPanel 1: Outcome Owl lists all possible results: \"No missing cases.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 2: Event Eagle circles favourable outcomes: \"These are the ones we want.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 3: Probability Pilot writes favourable over total: \"Keep it between 0 and 1.\"\r\n\r\nPanel 4: Complement Cat says, \"Not happening is `1 - happening`.\"\r\n",
